Create an archive from files and folders. Each input is stored under its own name (`src/`
becomes `src/…`). The format comes from `format` or the output's extension (`.zip`, `.7z`,
`.tar`, `.tar.zst`, `.tgz`, `.cpio.gz`, `.ar`, `.cab`, `.iso`, `.squashfs`, `.dmg`…); ar
and cab hold files only. An ISO has Joliet and Rock Ridge, a DMG is an HFS+ volume in a
zlib-compressed image. The archive is written to a temporary file and renamed into place,
so a failed or cancelled create leaves nothing behind.

Usage: beezip create [OPTIONS] <OUTPUT> <INPUTS>...

Arguments:
  <OUTPUT>
          The archive to write

  <INPUTS>...
          Files and folders to add

Options:
  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          The archive format [default: from the output's extension]
          
          [possible values: zip, 7z, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz, tar.lzma, tar.lz, tar.zst,
          tar.lz4, tar.br, tar.sz, tar.Z, tar.lzo, cpio, cpio.gz, cpio.bz2, cpio.xz, cpio.lzma,
          cpio.lz, cpio.zst, cpio.lz4, cpio.br, cpio.sz, cpio.Z, cpio.lzo, ar, cab, iso, squashfs,
          dmg]

  -m, --method <METHOD>
          The compression method: for zip `store`, `deflate` (the default), `bzip2`, `zstd`, `xz` or
          `ppmd`; for 7z `lzma2` (the default), `lzma`, `ppmd`, `bzip2`, `deflate` or `copy`; for
          cab `mszip` (the default) or `store`; for squashfs `gzip` (the default), `xz`, `zstd`,
          `lz4` or `lzo`. A tar's or cpio's codec comes from `format`
          
          [possible values: store, deflate, bzip2, zstd, xz, ppmd, lzma2, lzma, copy, mszip, gzip,
          lz4, lzo]

  -l, --level <LEVEL>
          The compression level; `formats.list` shows each codec's and method's range

      --threads <THREADS>
          Compression threads for codecs that have them; 0 is one per core [default: from config]

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          Encrypt the entries with this password (AES-256). Zip and 7z

      --encryption <ENCRYPTION>
          How to encrypt a zip [default: aes256]. `zipcrypto` is weak, for tools that can't read
          AES. Zip only

          Possible values:
          - aes256:    AES-256, the default
          - zipcrypto: The legacy scheme, for tools such as Windows Explorer that can't read AES.
            Weak

      --encrypt-headers[=<ENCRYPT_HEADERS>]
          Encrypt the file names too, so the archive can't even be listed without the password
          [default: true with a password]. 7z only
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --solid[=<SOLID>]
          Compress files together in solid blocks of up to 64 MiB (or four dictionaries, at most 1
          GiB): smaller archives, but reading one file decodes its block from the start [default:
          true]. 7z only
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --exclude <PATTERN>
          Leave out files and folders matching these patterns

  -L, --follow-links[=<FOLLOW_LINKS>]
          Store what symlinks point to instead of the links
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -v, --volume-size <SIZE>
          Split the archive into volumes of this size: `<output>.001`, `.002`… Bytes, or a size such
          as `100m`, `4g` or `700MB` (powers of 1024). At least 1 KiB

      --overwrite[=<OVERWRITE>]
          Replace the output if it exists
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --dry-run[=<DRY_RUN>]
          Show what would be added without writing anything
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
